    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, but it's still a bit abstract. I want to get even deeper into the gritty details to help the reader really 'see' them. When I say I want them to feel like a different kind of human, I'm thinking about more concrete, physical differences—like how they might look, their skin/hair texture, or even certain physical 'imperfections' caused by their environment that make them stand out from the 'perfect' looking Elites. Can you give me some more specific, raw descriptors for the Low-Tier people's physical appearance and maybe some specific cultural 'vices' or habits that would make them seem more 'unrefined' or 'primitive' compared to the Elites?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Understood, let's get very specific:\n\nLow-Tier individuals possess a rugged appearance; their skin shows brown patches from constant contact with damp, recycled materials, and some carry streaks of grime as a badge of resilience. Their hands, calloused from manual labor, exhibit scars, missing fingers, and a level of dexterity that contrasts starkly with the Elites' supposed 'gentle' technicity. Hair, a mix of long, unruly strands and short, piercing cuts, varies from one season to the next, as they work with nothing more than scraps and metal shavings. Faces are set with lines and squinting eyes, etched with soot and street smudges, a written history of shorter nights and toxic hobbies like urban exploration or scavenger hunts.\n\nCulturally, they indulge in raw, often raucous forms of entertainment, like illegal street circuits or abandoned base coliseums. Informal bartering dominates over the Elites’ prized exchanges in marketplaces. They favor empirical knowledge, leaning towards 'hands-on' learning, using experienced elders instead of the automated pedagogies of their floating counterparts. In the shadow of arcology ecosystems, they develop unique, makeshift technologies from scavenged parts; perhaps a luxury item",
      "source": "target",
